From dd4dd10611e8c23c7579a4ddb95655abebbaca27 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kevin Rajan <7121943+kvnloo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 03:59:49 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] fix(evals): postmortem log parsers count zero-hit and
 usage-unavailable calls

agent/turn_usage.py writes " cache=R/P" only when the provider read
something from cache, and logs "in=? out=? total=? ... usage=unavailable"
when a response carries no usage. Every reader of that line required
cache=, so every full miss was silently dropped: the logcalls lane
undercounted coverage and biased the overall hit ratio upward, most of
all around model switches and cache expiry, where misses cluster. The two
live cache probes dropped the same calls from their per-arm totals.

forensics/logcalls.py:
- cache= is optional; a line without it is a zero-hit call (hit=0).
- usage=unavailable lines are parsed and reported as
  coverage.usage_unavailable. They stay out of every token figure and out
  of the coverage fraction, matching state.db's api_call_count, which
  does not count them either.
- cache_state= is read when the line carries it; cache_state=no_field
  calls (no cache counter reported) stay out of the hit ratio, buckets
  and plateau pairs instead of reading as misses. Trailing fields such as
  ttfb= are tolerated.
- The coverage block gains zero_hit, no_cache_field and
  usage_unavailable, and the coverage print line shows them.

live_ab/cache_prefix_live.py, live_ab/cache_prefix_wire.py: the same
regex with the cache= group optional; a row without it reads cached=0.

Two harness self-tests cover the current line shapes and the pending
cache_state= / ttfb= shapes; both fail on the old regex.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
